WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:04.232
Welcome to Module 1 of the
Non-Person Identity learning path:

2
00:00:04.232 --> 00:00:06.825
inventory every non-person identity.

3
00:00:06.825 --> 00:00:08.988
A person leaves and HR tells you.

4
00:00:08.988 --> 00:00:11.988
A service account's owner
leaves and nothing tells you.

5
00:00:11.988 --> 00:00:16.424
This path builds that missing record,
through the six gates of the non-person

6
00:00:16.424 --> 00:00:18.325
identity implementation guide,

7
00:00:18.325 --> 00:00:21.775
with one module for each chapter
of the developer workbook.

8
00:00:21.775 --> 00:00:26.637
This module is Gate 1, Inventory,
with Chapter 1 of the workbook.

9
00:00:26.637 --> 00:00:31.800
You'll read every service account,
managed service account, app registration,

10
00:00:31.800 --> 00:00:35.753
service principal and managed
identity into one register,

11
00:00:35.753 --> 00:00:39.787
with its type, owners, credentials,
risk and candidate target.

12
00:00:39.787 --> 00:00:41.300
You decide nothing yet.

13
00:00:41.300 --> 00:00:44.762
The code runs on your laptop,
against a fictional estate.

14
00:00:44.762 --> 00:00:50.120
It's illustrative: tested against
fixtures, not run against a live tenant.

15
00:00:53.870 --> 00:00:55.483
Why start with an inventory?

16
00:00:55.483 --> 00:00:58.396
Because the list is always
longer than you think.

17
00:00:58.396 --> 00:01:03.852
In Entra, consent creates service principals,
and Microsoft's own services create

18
00:01:03.852 --> 00:01:08.907
more, so the inventory is always larger
than the list of apps anyone asked for.

19
00:01:08.907 --> 00:01:13.483
And in Active Directory, no attribute
marks a user as a service account.

20
00:01:13.483 --> 00:01:18.877
Microsoft Learn's guidance on securing
user-based service accounts finds candidates

21
00:01:18.877 --> 00:01:21.349
by three signs: a service principal name,

22
00:01:21.349 --> 00:01:24.795
trust for delegation, and a
password that never expires.

23
00:01:24.795 --> 00:01:27.220
And it warns that this
might not find them all.

24
00:01:27.220 --> 00:01:32.074
So in this module you read every
non-person identity into one register,

25
00:01:32.074 --> 00:01:37.095
with its type, its owners, its credentials,
its risk and a candidate target.

26
00:01:37.095 --> 00:01:39.020
You don't decide anything yet.

27
00:01:39.020 --> 00:01:41.950
Inventory first; decide later.

28
00:01:45.700 --> 00:01:48.540
Here's what you'll be able to
do by the end of this module,

29
00:01:48.540 --> 00:01:51.187
which takes about fifty-five
minutes with the lab.

30
00:01:51.187 --> 00:01:56.375
You'll name the identity types the register
holds, and where each one is read from.

31
00:01:56.375 --> 00:02:00.277
You'll explain the program's
high, medium and low risk rule,

32
00:02:00.277 --> 00:02:03.100
and why a stale account
is only ever a lead.

33
00:02:03.100 --> 00:02:07.725
You'll run the inventory against the
fixtures and read its high-risk findings.

34
00:02:07.725 --> 00:02:10.436
And you'll show that the
reader refuses a call the

35
00:02:10.436 --> 00:02:13.012
task doesn't declare, and
a cloud it doesn't know.

36
00:02:13.012 --> 00:02:17.942
Before you start, read the guide's
track at a glance: for this track,

37
00:02:17.942 --> 00:02:22.313
the system inventory or C M D B
is the source of truth, not HR.

38
00:02:22.313 --> 00:02:26.150
And review Microsoft Automation
Module 1 on permissions.

39
00:02:26.150 --> 00:02:32.250
Lab 1 needs PowerShell 7.1 or later with
Pester 5, and a clone of the repository.

40
00:02:32.250 --> 00:02:33.540
No tenant.

41
00:02:37.290 --> 00:02:41.165
Unit 1 is the guide's Step
1, which has six sub-steps.

42
00:02:41.165 --> 00:02:43.857
One a: Active Directory service accounts,

43
00:02:43.857 --> 00:02:47.815
managed service accounts, and
the group and delegated kinds.

44
00:02:47.815 --> 00:02:52.238
Stale is a lead, not a verdict, and
for a group managed account you record

45
00:02:52.238 --> 00:02:56.965
which hosts can retrieve its password,
because that's its real blast radius.

46
00:02:56.965 --> 00:03:00.257
One b: app registrations
and service principals,

47
00:03:00.257 --> 00:03:02.890
with each one's service principal type.

48
00:03:02.890 --> 00:03:08.154
One c: managed identities and their role
assignments; a user-assigned identity

49
00:03:08.154 --> 00:03:12.690
outlives the resources it's attached
to, so it's the type that orphans.

50
00:03:12.690 --> 00:03:17.703
One d: every federated credential's
issuer, subject and audience.

51
00:03:17.703 --> 00:03:23.277
One e: AI agent identities and their
sponsors, where the tenant exposes them.

52
00:03:23.277 --> 00:03:27.878
And one f joins each identity to
its record in the system inventory,

53
00:03:27.878 --> 00:03:30.303
with two human owners and a sponsor.

54
00:03:30.303 --> 00:03:35.960
The validation kit's read-only checks
one, six, seven and eight prove the gate.

55
00:03:39.710 --> 00:03:42.085
Unit 2 is the register as code.

56
00:03:42.085 --> 00:03:45.063
It reads two collections
from Active Directory,

57
00:03:45.063 --> 00:03:48.935
nine from Microsoft Graph, and
one from Azure Resource Manager.

58
00:03:48.935 --> 00:03:52.337
From the directory: the user
accounts used as services,

59
00:03:52.337 --> 00:03:57.252
found with Learn's three criteria and
the naming convention in one server-side

60
00:03:57.252 --> 00:04:00.579
query; and the managed service
accounts of every kind,

61
00:04:00.579 --> 00:04:02.847
with who may retrieve each password.

62
00:04:02.847 --> 00:04:07.731
From Graph: app registrations with
their owners and federated credentials;

63
00:04:07.731 --> 00:04:12.847
service principals by type; Graph's own app
roles, so a permission shows up by name;

64
00:04:12.847 --> 00:04:18.273
and the delegated consent grants, where
tenant-wide consent shows as all principals.

65
00:04:18.273 --> 00:04:21.812
And one read people forget:
a user-assigned identity's

66
00:04:21.812 --> 00:04:25.834
federated credentials live in
Resource Manager, not in Graph.

67
00:04:25.834 --> 00:04:29.003
Every identity gets one
row with the same columns,

68
00:04:29.003 --> 00:04:33.000
whatever its type, so the whole
register lands in one file.

69
00:04:36.750 --> 00:04:39.159
Every call goes through one reader,

70
00:04:39.159 --> 00:04:43.062
built for one task in one cloud,
and it enforces five rules.

71
00:04:43.062 --> 00:04:48.025
Only declared calls: the configuration
file lists each call with its permission,

72
00:04:48.025 --> 00:04:52.025
and a call the task doesn't declare
throws before anything is sent.

73
00:04:52.025 --> 00:04:53.743
Reads only: it sends GET,

74
00:04:53.743 --> 00:04:58.175
and runs only the directory Get
cmdlets the call list names.

75
00:04:58.175 --> 00:05:01.655
One host: every URL,
including every next link,

76
00:05:01.655 --> 00:05:07.675
must be on the cloud's own Graph or Resource
Manager host, or the token isn't sent.

77
00:05:07.675 --> 00:05:12.925
One tenant: a live run refuses a session
in another cloud or another tenant.

78
00:05:12.925 --> 00:05:16.262
And every page: collections
are read to the end.

79
00:05:16.262 --> 00:05:18.237
One permission deserves a note.

80
00:05:18.237 --> 00:05:22.942
The inventory holds Directory dot Read dot
All only because Microsoft Learn's page

81
00:05:22.942 --> 00:05:27.851
for listing delegated permission grants
names it as the least-privileged permission.

82
00:05:27.851 --> 00:05:32.730
It's the broadest read the inventory
holds, and that's the only reason.

83
00:05:36.480 --> 00:05:40.420
Risk, the candidate target and the
stale lead come from the program,

84
00:05:40.420 --> 00:05:43.142
not from Microsoft, so
they're design guidance.

85
00:05:43.142 --> 00:05:47.983
An enabled identity is high risk for a
password that never expires or hasn't changed

86
00:05:47.983 --> 00:05:52.473
in more than three hundred sixty-five
days, membership of a privileged Active

87
00:05:52.473 --> 00:05:57.243
Directory group, unconstrained delegation,
or a high-privilege Graph permission.

88
00:05:57.243 --> 00:06:01.046
Other enabled traditional
accounts, managed service accounts,

89
00:06:01.046 --> 00:06:05.567
legacy service principals and anything
holding a client secret are medium.

90
00:06:05.567 --> 00:06:06.992
Everything else is low.

91
00:06:06.992 --> 00:06:11.680
The target is only a candidate;
Module 3 makes the decision.

92
00:06:11.680 --> 00:06:16.316
A stale lead is an enabled directory
account with no logon in ninety days,

93
00:06:16.316 --> 00:06:19.794
and it's only a lead, because
Microsoft Learn documents

94
00:06:19.794 --> 00:06:23.580
that the last logon timestamp
isn't updated at every sign-in.

95
00:06:23.580 --> 00:06:27.705
Confirm it against the domain
controller and Entra sign-in logs.

96
00:06:27.705 --> 00:06:31.555
The code never disables,
moves or deletes anything.

97
00:06:31.555 --> 00:06:36.660
And SQL Server and LDAP-bind accounts
are handed to the Data Platform plan.

98
00:06:40.410 --> 00:06:46.860
The register becomes findings, under nine
rules, N P I I N V oh one to oh nine.

99
00:06:46.860 --> 00:06:50.458
Only the first is a hard
finding: a high-risk identity,

100
00:06:50.458 --> 00:06:53.135
with its reasons and candidate target.

101
00:06:53.135 --> 00:06:56.298
The rest are warnings to
act on, or information.

102
00:06:56.298 --> 00:06:58.448
Oh two is a stale lead to confirm.

103
00:06:58.448 --> 00:07:01.062
Oh three is an app registration or legacy

104
00:07:01.062 --> 00:07:03.984
service principal with
fewer than two owners.

105
00:07:03.984 --> 00:07:09.410
Oh four is a client secret, and oh five an
expired credential still on the object.

106
00:07:09.410 --> 00:07:13.323
Oh six is a delegated grant
consented for all users.

107
00:07:13.323 --> 00:07:18.347
Oh seven, information only, is an
account handed to the Data Platform plan.

108
00:07:18.347 --> 00:07:21.365
Oh eight is an account
found only by a password

109
00:07:21.365 --> 00:07:23.985
that never expires, which may be a person.

110
00:07:23.985 --> 00:07:27.072
And oh nine is a type the
register doesn't classify.

111
00:07:27.072 --> 00:07:32.760
Every finding has a stable I D, so the
same gap keeps its name from run to run.

112
00:07:36.510 --> 00:07:37.235
Try it.

113
00:07:37.235 --> 00:07:41.566
Lab 1 runs in the non-person
identity folder of the examples,

114
00:07:41.566 --> 00:07:44.748
in PowerShell 7, against
a fictional estate.

115
00:07:44.748 --> 00:07:47.760
Nothing signs in to anything
or sends a request.

116
00:07:47.760 --> 00:07:53.047
Step one runs Learn's own samples,
exactly as published, against stand-ins.

117
00:07:53.047 --> 00:07:58.697
Twenty pass, none fail, and ten aren't
run, because those ten are Chapter 2's.

118
00:07:58.697 --> 00:08:01.285
Step two lists what the inventory needs.

119
00:08:01.285 --> 00:08:06.535
Two Graph permissions: Application dot Read
dot All and Directory dot Read dot All.

120
00:08:06.535 --> 00:08:08.073
The Reader role in Azure.

121
00:08:08.073 --> 00:08:13.723
And in Active Directory, only the default
read every authenticated domain user has.

122
00:08:13.723 --> 00:08:16.098
Notice that nothing ends in ReadWrite.

123
00:08:16.098 --> 00:08:18.159
The inventory can't change anything,

124
00:08:18.159 --> 00:08:22.840
and that's what you'd show an assessor
who asks what the reading process may do.

125
00:08:26.590 --> 00:08:30.427
Step three runs the inventory
against the fictional estate.

126
00:08:30.427 --> 00:08:34.839
It reads thirty-five identities,
fifteen from Active Directory and

127
00:08:34.839 --> 00:08:39.328
twenty from Entra, and rates eight
high, eight medium and nineteen low.

128
00:08:39.328 --> 00:08:43.322
That's eight findings, eleven
warnings and three information items,

129
00:08:43.322 --> 00:08:46.389
and three accounts handed
to the Data Platform plan.

130
00:08:46.389 --> 00:08:51.478
The manifest records the tenant, every
request, and a hash of every file.

131
00:08:51.478 --> 00:08:54.453
Step four reads the eight
high-risk identities.

132
00:08:54.453 --> 00:08:59.063
Three accounts have passwords that
never expire, and one of them,

133
00:08:59.063 --> 00:09:02.891
svc oldftp, is also a stale
lead, so verify its use first.

134
00:09:02.891 --> 00:09:06.534
j doe was found only by a
password that never expires,

135
00:09:06.534 --> 00:09:09.415
so it may be a person,
not a service account.

136
00:09:09.415 --> 00:09:12.537
Others sit in Backup
Operators or Domain Admins,

137
00:09:12.537 --> 00:09:15.503
or are trusted for
unconstrained delegation.

138
00:09:15.503 --> 00:09:19.640
And the Mail Automation app holds
a directory-wide write permission.

139
00:09:23.390 --> 00:09:25.890
Step five shows the
reader's rules at work.

140
00:09:25.890 --> 00:09:29.726
You build a reader for the inventory
task, with the fixture invoker

141
00:09:29.726 --> 00:09:33.840
standing in for the network, and ask it
for a call the task doesn't declare.

142
00:09:33.840 --> 00:09:35.922
It refuses before anything is sent,

143
00:09:35.922 --> 00:09:39.728
and tells you to add the call to
the call list with its permission.

144
00:09:39.728 --> 00:09:44.683
Then you ask for the cloud G C C in
capitals, and it refuses that too:

145
00:09:44.683 --> 00:09:49.827
the cloud names are global, gcc,
gcchigh and dod, and it never guesses.

146
00:09:49.827 --> 00:09:53.315
Step six reads the hand-off
to the Data Platform plan.

147
00:09:53.315 --> 00:09:59.195
Two accounts go by their SQL Server
service principal names, svc sqlprod and

148
00:09:59.195 --> 00:10:04.452
the group managed account gmsa sql, and
one, svc ldapbind, goes by its name.

149
00:10:04.452 --> 00:10:09.489
Step seven runs every test: five
hundred thirteen pass, none fail.

150
00:10:09.489 --> 00:10:11.690
Then delete the output folder.

151
00:10:15.440 --> 00:10:19.365
Lab 2 runs the same inventory
against your own test tenant.

152
00:10:19.365 --> 00:10:22.807
You need a test tenant in
the worldwide or G C C cloud,

153
00:10:22.807 --> 00:10:27.315
with a test Azure subscription,
and a test Active Directory domain.

154
00:10:27.315 --> 00:10:30.040
Never production, and
only fictional people.

155
00:10:30.040 --> 00:10:33.515
Register a reading app with
a certificate, not a secret.

156
00:10:33.515 --> 00:10:37.766
Grant it Application dot Read dot
All and Directory dot Read dot All,

157
00:10:37.766 --> 00:10:39.815
and Reader on one resource group.

158
00:10:39.815 --> 00:10:41.384
Then run the inventory live,

159
00:10:41.384 --> 00:10:44.877
and compare the register with
the lists in the admin center.

160
00:10:44.877 --> 00:10:47.584
That comparison is what
tells you whether a real

161
00:10:47.584 --> 00:10:50.290
tenant returns the shapes
the fixtures assumed.

162
00:10:50.290 --> 00:10:54.980
To back it out, step eight removes the
Reader assignment and the two permissions,

163
00:10:54.980 --> 00:10:59.315
deletes the app registration and its
certificate, and deletes the output.

164
00:10:59.315 --> 00:11:01.465
The authors haven't run Lab 2.

165
00:11:01.465 --> 00:11:04.790
The workbook's Chapter 1
has the exact commands.

166
00:11:08.540 --> 00:11:09.927
The federal requirement.

167
00:11:09.927 --> 00:11:13.468
Gate 1 meets NIST
special publication eight

168
00:11:13.468 --> 00:11:17.103
hundred fifty-three A C two,
I A four and C M eight.

169
00:11:17.103 --> 00:11:20.340
The module's table maps
each one to its evidence.

170
00:11:20.340 --> 00:11:24.246
For C M eight, system component
inventory, it's the register,

171
00:11:24.246 --> 00:11:28.640
as JSON and as a spreadsheet file,
and the Data Platform hand-off.

172
00:11:28.640 --> 00:11:31.307
For account and identifier management,

173
00:11:31.307 --> 00:11:34.702
it's the register's identity,
type and owner columns.

174
00:11:34.702 --> 00:11:39.252
For privileged accounts and authenticator
management, the high-risk findings.

175
00:11:39.252 --> 00:11:42.251
For least privilege of the
reading process itself,

176
00:11:42.251 --> 00:11:44.465
the permission list and the call list.

177
00:11:44.465 --> 00:11:47.278
And for the run's integrity, the manifest,

178
00:11:47.278 --> 00:11:51.540
which holds the tenant, every
request, and a hash of every file.

179
00:11:51.540 --> 00:11:56.178
K S I's are FedRAMP twenty X indicators
for cloud service providers.

180
00:11:56.178 --> 00:12:00.618
The agency lines its evidence up
with what its providers report;

181
00:12:00.618 --> 00:12:02.628
it doesn't report K S I's itself.

182
00:12:02.628 --> 00:12:06.250
Which file supports which
indicator is the program's mapping.

183
00:12:10.000 --> 00:12:11.975
Check yourself with two questions.

184
00:12:11.975 --> 00:12:17.075
First: an enabled Active Directory
account has had no logon for ninety days.

185
00:12:17.075 --> 00:12:18.337
What does the code do?

186
00:12:18.337 --> 00:12:19.562
The answer is b.

187
00:12:19.562 --> 00:12:24.048
It raises a stale lead, N P I I N
V oh two, to confirm against the

188
00:12:24.048 --> 00:12:29.001
domain controller and Entra sign-in
logs, and it changes nothing.

189
00:12:29.001 --> 00:12:31.109
A stale account is only a lead,

190
00:12:31.109 --> 00:12:35.062
and the code never disables,
moves or deletes anything.

191
00:12:35.062 --> 00:12:41.100
Second: j doe is high risk, and
also carries N P I I N V oh eight.

192
00:12:41.100 --> 00:12:42.288
Why does that matter?

193
00:12:42.288 --> 00:12:43.424
The answer is a.

194
00:12:43.424 --> 00:12:46.732
It was found only by a
password that never expires,

195
00:12:46.732 --> 00:12:49.475
so it may be a person,
not a service account.

196
00:12:49.475 --> 00:12:51.515
Learn's criteria can find people too,

197
00:12:51.515 --> 00:12:55.663
so someone confirms it before the next
module applies the ownership rule.

198
00:12:55.663 --> 00:12:58.089
The module page has three more questions,

199
00:12:58.089 --> 00:13:00.940
each linked to the
section that answers it.

200
00:13:04.690 --> 00:13:05.515
To sum up.

201
00:13:05.515 --> 00:13:10.124
You read a fictional estate of
thirty-five identities into one register,

202
00:13:10.124 --> 00:13:14.656
found the eight high-risk ones, saw
the reader refuse an undeclared call,

203
00:13:14.656 --> 00:13:19.802
and handed three SQL Server and LDAP-bind
accounts to the Data Platform plan.

204
00:13:19.802 --> 00:13:24.456
The evidence an ISSO keeps for Gate
1 from this module is the register,

205
00:13:24.456 --> 00:13:28.456
the findings, the hand-off and
the manifest that hashes them,

206
00:13:28.456 --> 00:13:32.702
all dated; the permission list from
step two; and the test result.

207
00:13:32.702 --> 00:13:35.777
Before you ship, work through
the chapter's checklist.

208
00:13:35.777 --> 00:13:40.290
Next is Module 2: owners,
sponsors and the orphan rule.

209
00:13:40.290 --> 00:13:44.553
Every identity in this register
needs two human owners and a sponsor,

210
00:13:44.553 --> 00:13:49.090
and Module 2 checks who they are and
what happens when there's nobody left.
